  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nA large closed low continues to spin off the CA coast with a ridge \nover the Southern Plains. A shortwave overhead rounding that ridge \nis responsible for widely scattered showers that remain across \ncentral and eastern KS early this morning. Once the upper wave \npasses to our east mid to late morning, that should end any \nlingering showers with subsidence taking over. That should also \nallow clouds to clear out with temperatures a little bit warmer than \nyesterday, reaching the mid to upper 70s. The upper ridge axis then \nmoves overhead for Thursday and Friday, leading to slightly higher \nmid-level heights and increasing southerly winds, especially on \nFriday as lee sfc troughing deepens in response to the approaching \nupper trough to the west. While the spread in temperatures remains \nsmall, am thinking the 90th percentile is probably more accurate as \nmodels oftentimes are not warm enough in setups like these and we've \nalready been seeing that trend the last few days, even if only by a \nfew degrees. With that in mind, would think some places will \nprobably see low 80s at some point between today and Saturday.\n\nMore of the guidance seems to be catching on to the slower trend \nwith the upper trough and associated cold front. The lead shortwave \non Friday looks to eject north/northeast of the area and phase with \nthe northern stream troughing pattern in Canada, limiting its impact \non our area. What remains of the southern stream looks to move \nacross the Four Corners region Sunday and eventually through the \nPlains Monday, bringing the front through the area sometime Monday. \nThere are still enough timing differences that result in large \ntemperature spreads starting Sunday in north central KS if the front \nwere to move faster and bring clouds/rain into the area that early. \nA slower solution would keep warmer/drier conditions area-wide \nthrough Sunday with better rain chances Monday, followed by cooler \ntemperatures back towards average early next week. As of now, the \nhigher PoPs (30-50%) are focused on Monday, which makes sense based \non the recent trend.",
